    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Critics argue that UBI often functions as a \"band-aid\" that fails to address the root cause of wealth inequality by providing a static payout that the market does not recognize as productivity. Proponents frequently ignore the opportunity cost of tax revenue, assuming it can be retired without increasing debt or depressing labor demand for beneficial jobs. The most damaging holes in their theory lie in the failure to incentivize education, entrepreneurship, and essential care work that currently holds jobs viable. By treating the economy as purely defined by consumption rather than production, UBI ultimately harms the very workforce it is supposed to protect against technological displacement. You should challenge them to explain an economic model where universal welfare does not inherently reduce the aggregate incentive to work, innovate, or produce goods.",
